Task 1: Set up Dogtag CA for Certificate Management
Subtask 1.1: Verify IdM Server Installation and Dogtag Integration
First, let's verify that your IdM server is properly installed with Dogtag CA integration.

Check IdM server status:
sudo ipactl status
Verify Dogtag CA service is running:
sudo systemctl status pki-tomcatd@pki-tomcat.service
Check CA subsystem status:
sudo pki-server status pki-tomcat
Subtask 1.2: Access Dogtag CA Web Interface
Open the Dogtag CA web interface:
# Get your server's IP address
ip addr show | grep inet
Access the CA interface via browser:
URL: https://your-server-ip:8443/ca
Accept the self-signed certificate warning
Login with your IdM admin credentials
Subtask 1.3: Configure CA Certificate Profiles
List existing certificate profiles:
ipa certprofile-find
Create a custom certificate profile for web servers:
# Create a profile configuration file
cat > /tmp/webserver-profile.cfg << 'EOF'
profileId=caIPAserviceCert
classId=caEnrollImpl
desc=This certificate profile is for enrolling server certificates with IPA-RA agent authentication.
visible=true
enable=true
enableBy=admin
auth.instance_id=raCertAuth
name=IPA-RA Agent-Authenticated Server Certificate Enrollment
input.list=i1,i2
input.i1.class_id=certReqInputImpl
input.i2.class_id=submitterInfoInputImpl
output.list=o1
output.o1.class_id=certOutputImpl
policyset.list=serverCertSet
policyset.serverCertSet.list=1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8
policyset.serverCertSet.1.constraint.class_id=subjectNameConstraintImpl
policyset.serverCertSet.1.constraint.name=Subject Name Constraint
policyset.serverCertSet.1.constraint.params.pattern=CN=[^,]+,.+
policyset.serverCertSet.1.constraint.params.accept=true
policyset.serverCertSet.1.default.class_id=authTokenSubjectNameDefaultImpl
policyset.serverCertSet.1.default.name=Subject Name Default
policyset.serverCertSet.1.default.params.name=CN=$request.req_subject_name.cn$, O=EXAMPLE.COM
EOF
Import the certificate profile:
ipa certprofile-import webserver-custom --file=/tmp/webserver-profile.cfg --desc="Custom Web Server Certificate Profile" --store=true
Subtask 1.4: Configure Certificate Authority Settings
Check current CA configuration:
ipa config-show | grep -i cert
Configure certificate validity period:
# Set default certificate validity to 2 years
ipa config-mod --maxusername=32 --maxhostname=64
Verify CA certificate chain:
ipa ca-show ipa
Task 2: Issue and Manage Certificates Using ipa cert-request
Subtask 2.1: Generate Certificate Signing Request (CSR)
Create a private key for a web server:
# Create directory for certificates
mkdir -p /home/student/certificates
cd /home/student/certificates

# Generate private key
openssl genrsa -out webserver.key 2048
Create a Certificate Signing Request:
# Create CSR configuration file
cat > webserver.conf << 'EOF'
[req]
distinguished_name = req_distinguished_name
req_extensions = v3_req
prompt = no

[req_distinguished_name]
C = US
ST = California
L = San Francisco
O = Example Organization
OU = IT Department
CN = webserver.example.com

[v3_req]
keyUsage = keyEncipherment, dataEncipherment
extendedKeyUsage = serverAuth
subjectAltName = @alt_names

[alt_names]
DNS.1 = webserver.example.com
DNS.2 = www.example.com
IP.1 = 192.168.1.100
EOF

# Generate CSR
openssl req -new -key webserver.key -out webserver.csr -config webserver.conf
Verify the CSR content:
openssl req -in webserver.csr -text -noout
Subtask 2.2: Submit Certificate Request to IdM CA
Submit the certificate request:
# Submit CSR to IdM CA
ipa cert-request webserver.csr --principal=HTTP/webserver.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM --profile-id=caIPAserviceCert
Check certificate request status:
# List pending certificate requests
ipa cert-find --status=VALID --subject="webserver.example.com"
Retrieve the issued certificate:
# Get certificate by serial number (replace XXXX with actual serial number)
ipa cert-show XXXX --out=/home/student/certificates/webserver.crt
Subtask 2.3: Issue User Certificates
Create a user certificate request:
# Generate user private key
openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048

# Create user CSR
cat > user.conf << 'EOF'
[req]
distinguished_name = req_distinguished_name
prompt = no

[req_distinguished_name]
C = US
ST = California
L = San Francisco
O = Example Organization
OU = Users
CN = john.doe
emailAddress = john.doe@example.com
EOF

# Generate user CSR
openssl req -new -key user.key -out user.csr -config user.conf
Submit user certificate request:
# Submit user certificate request
ipa cert-request user.csr --principal=john.doe@EXAMPLE.COM
Subtask 2.4: Manage Certificate Lifecycle
List all certificates:
# Show all certificates
ipa cert-find
Check certificate details:
# View specific certificate details
ipa cert-show SERIAL_NUMBER --all
Revoke a certificate if needed:
# Revoke certificate (use actual serial number)
ipa cert-revoke SERIAL_NUMBER --revocation-reason=cessationOfOperation
Check certificate revocation status:
# Verify revocation
ipa cert-show SERIAL_NUMBER | grep -i revoked
Task 3: Configure Automatic Certificate Renewal
Subtask 3.1: Set Up Certmonger for Automatic Renewal
Install and configure certmonger:
# Certmonger should already be installed, but verify
sudo dnf install certmonger -y

# Start and enable certmonger service
sudo systemctl start certmonger
sudo systemctl enable certmonger
Check certmonger status:
sudo systemctl status certmonger
List current certificate tracking:
sudo getcert list
Subtask 3.2: Configure Automatic Renewal for Web Server Certificate
Add certificate to certmonger tracking:
# Add webserver certificate for automatic renewal
sudo getcert start-tracking \
    -c IPA \
    -f /home/student/certificates/webserver.crt \
    -k /home/student/certificates/webserver.key \
    -I webserver-cert \
    -N "CN=webserver.example.com,O=EXAMPLE.COM" \
    -D webserver.example.com \
    -K HTTP/webserver.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM
Verify certificate tracking:
sudo getcert list -i webserver-cert
Check renewal configuration:
# View detailed tracking information
sudo getcert list -v
Subtask 3.3: Configure Renewal Notifications
Set up email notifications for certificate renewal:
# Configure certmonger to send notifications
sudo getcert start-tracking \
    -c IPA \
    -f /home/student/certificates/webserver.crt \
    -k /home/student/certificates/webserver.key \
    -I webserver-cert-notify \
    -N "CN=webserver.example.com,O=EXAMPLE.COM" \
    -B "/usr/bin/systemctl reload httpd" \
    -A "/usr/bin/systemctl reload httpd"
Test certificate renewal process:
# Force certificate renewal for testing
sudo getcert resubmit -i webserver-cert
Monitor renewal logs:
# Check certmonger logs
sudo journalctl -u certmonger -f
Subtask 3.4: Configure System Certificate Renewal
Check system certificates managed by certmonger:
# List all tracked certificates
sudo getcert list | grep -E "(Request ID|status|expires)"
Verify IdM server certificates are tracked:
# Check IdM server certificate tracking
sudo getcert list | grep -i ipa
Configure renewal hooks for services:
# Example: Configure Apache restart after certificate renewal
sudo getcert start-tracking \
    -c IPA \
    -f /etc/pki/tls/certs/server.crt \
    -k /etc/pki/tls/private/server.key \
    -I apache-server-cert \
    -r \
    -B "/usr/bin/systemctl reload httpd"
Verification and Testing
Verify Certificate Management Setup
Test certificate validation:
# Verify certificate chain
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/ipa/ca.crt /home/student/certificates/webserver.crt
Check certificate expiration dates:
# Check when certificates expire
openssl x509 -in /home/student/certificates/webserver.crt -noout -dates
Test automatic renewal functionality:
# Check certmonger renewal status
sudo getcert list | grep -A 5 -B 5 "expires"
Validate Dogtag CA Integration
Verify CA subsystem health:
# Check CA subsystem status
sudo pki-server subsystem-show -i pki-tomcat ca
Test certificate issuance workflow:
# Verify end-to-end certificate issuance
ipa cert-find --subject="webserver.example.com" --all
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Certificate Request Fails
Symptoms: Certificate request returns error or stays in pending status

Solution:

# Check IdM CA status
sudo ipactl status

# Restart CA services if needed
sudo systemctl restart pki-tomcatd@pki-tomcat.service

# Check CA logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/pki/pki-tomcat/ca/debug
Issue 2: Automatic Renewal Not Working
Symptoms: Certificates expire without renewal

Solution:

# Check certmonger service
sudo systemctl status certmonger

# Review certmonger logs
sudo journalctl -u certmonger --since "1 day ago"

# Manually trigger renewal
sudo getcert resubmit -i CERT_ID
Issue 3: Dogtag Web Interface Inaccessible
Symptoms: Cannot access CA web interface

Solution:

# Check Tomcat service
sudo systemctl status pki-tomcatd@pki-tomcat.service

# Verify firewall settings
sudo firewall-cmd --list-ports
sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=8443/tcp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Check SELinux context
sudo setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect 1
